# HTTPS front for the Docker Compose stack — MULTI-WORKSPACE (Shape 2) mode.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM Caddyfile.domain. That file declares one site address,
# `{$HTTPS_DOMAIN}`, and Caddy serves ONLY the addresses it is given — so `acme.example.com` does not
# reach the stack at all, no matter what `ROOT_DOMAIN` says on the API. A multi-workspace deployment
# needs the apex AND the wildcard, which is what this adds. Select it per deployment:
#
#     HTTPS_DOMAIN=example.com  CADDYFILE=Caddyfile.domain-wildcard  docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.https.yml up -d
#
# It is a separate file rather than a commented block in the other one because getting a wildcard
# certificate is a real decision with a prerequisite (below), and a deployment that is working today
# on a single domain must not be able to inherit that prerequisite by accident.
#
# Validated with `caddy validate` against caddy:2-alpine, both as shipped and with the `tls` line
# below uncommented — both report "Valid configuration".
#
# ── THE PREREQUISITE, STATED PLAINLY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Let's Encrypt will not issue a wildcard over the HTTP-01 challenge. Wildcards require DNS-01, and
# DNS-01 requires Caddy to hold an API credential for your DNS provider — which means a Caddy build
# containing that provider's module. The stock `caddy:2-alpine` image in docker-compose.https.yml
# contains none, so AS SHIPPED this file's automatic path cannot obtain the certificate.
#
# There are two honest ways forward. Pick one before switching CADDYFILE, not after.
#
#   A. BRING YOUR OWN WILDCARD CERTIFICATE — works with the stock image, today. Put the pair at
#      deploy/caddy/certs/ (already mounted read-only at /certs by docker-compose.https.yml) and
#      uncomment the `tls` line below. Renewal is then yours to arrange, exactly as it already is
#      for the Caddyfile.local self-signed path.
#
#   B. BUILD A CADDY WITH YOUR DNS PROVIDER'S MODULE and let it renew automatically:
#
#        FROM caddy:2-builder AS build
#        RUN xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare   # or route53, azure, gcloud…
#        FROM caddy:2-alpine
#        COPY --from=build /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy
#
#      Point the `caddy` service at that image, pass the provider's token in, and replace the `tls`
#      line with your provider's directive, e.g. `tls { dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN} }`.
#
# ── AND THE TWO THINGS THE API STILL NEEDS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  1. `ROOT_DOMAIN` in the root `.env` must equal HTTPS_DOMAIN, or the API will not derive workspace
#     slugs from these hostnames. `install.sh` / `install.ps1` now ask for it; the API prints a boot
#     ERROR if it disagrees with APP_BASE_URL.
#  2. `TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2` — this Caddy, then the web container's nginx. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
#
# Host is NOT rewritten anywhere in this chain, and that is what makes the whole thing work: Caddy's
# `reverse_proxy` passes Host through untouched, and the web container's nginx sets
# `proxy_set_header Host $host` explicitly (apps/web/nginx.conf.template). The API derives the
# workspace from that header and from nothing else, so any hop that replaced it would route every
# customer to DEFAULT_ORG_SLUG silently. Verify after deploying, rather than trusting this comment:
# `GET /api/platform-admin/routing` reports `observed.resolvedSlug` for the request you just made.

# The apex serves the workspace finder ("I know my email, not my workspace address"); every
# subdomain serves that workspace. Both are the same upstream — the API tells them apart by Host.
{$HTTPS_DOMAIN}, *.{$HTTPS_DOMAIN} {
	encode gzip

	# Option A: your own wildcard certificate. Uncomment with the pair in deploy/caddy/certs/.
	# tls /certs/cert.pem /certs/key.pem

	# Option B: automatic DNS-01 with a DNS-provider module compiled in. Replace with yours.
	# tls {
	#	dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}
	# }

	# includeSubDomains is not optional here, unlike on a single-domain deployment: every customer
	# IS a subdomain, so omitting it would leave every real customer hostname unprotected while the
	# apex looked covered. Same reasoning and same two years as Caddyfile.domain; `preload` is
	# likewise left off, because committing a domain to the browser-baked list is not this repo's
	# call to make for whatever domain an operator points at it.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains"

	reverse_proxy web:80
}
